Objective

Build and tune custom Wazuh detection rules for two distinct attack categories:

SSH brute-force login attempts — host-based detection using authentication logs.
Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) against DVWA — web-based detection using Apache access logs.

The objective is to validate that each rule fires correctly and maps to the appropriate MITRE ATT&CK technique.

Lab Environment
Machine	OS	Purpose
Ubuntu 1	Ubuntu 24.04 LTS	Wazuh Manager (local_rules.xml lives here)
Ubuntu 2	Ubuntu 24.04 LTS	Wazuh Agent + DVWA (attack surface)
Part 1 — SSH Brute Force Detection
Step 1: First attempt — a rule that looked right but wasn't

The initial rule chained off Wazuh's default SSH authentication-failure rule (5760) without a frequency threshold:

<rule id="100020" level="12">
  <if_sid>5760</if_sid>
  <description>Custom: SSH authentication failed</description>
  <mitre><id>T1110.001</id></mitre>
  <group>authentication_failed,ssh,bruteforce,attack</group>
</rule>

Problem identified: This rule fired on every single failed login, including one-off password typos, rather than detecting an actual burst of failed attempts.

Tagging a single authentication failure as brute force (T1110.001) was inaccurate and could have flooded the dashboard with false positives.

Step 2: Corrected rule with frequency/timeframe correlation

The rule was changed to require multiple failures from the same source within a defined time period:

<rule id="100020" level="12" frequency="6" timeframe="120">
  <if_matched_sid>5760</if_matched_sid>
  <same_source_ip />
  <description>Custom: SSH brute force - 6 failed logins in 2 min</description>
  <mitre><id>T1110.001</id></mitre>
  <group>authentication_failed,ssh,bruteforce,attack,</group>
</rule>

frequency="6" and timeframe="120" require six matches from the same source IP within 120 seconds before the custom rule fires.

This allows the rule to detect a brute-force pattern rather than treating isolated authentication failures as brute-force activity.

Step 3: Test

The rule was tested by manually generating six or more failed SSH login attempts within two minutes from a test source.

The Wazuh Dashboard confirmed that:

The underlying authentication-failure rule (5760) detected the individual failures.
The custom rule (100020) fired after the required frequency threshold was reached.
The custom alert represented the correlated brute-force activity rather than a single failed login.
Part 2 — Reflected XSS Detection
Step 1: Rule definition

A custom rule was created for reflected XSS activity against DVWA:

<rule id="100012" level="8">
  <if_sid>31105</if_sid>
  <description>meduilm-HIGH: XSS attempt detected against monitored application</description>
  <group>attack,web,xss_attempt,</group>
</rule>

The rule chains from Wazuh's stock web-attack rule (31105), which provides the underlying XSS pattern matching against the Apache access log.

Step 2: Test

The test was performed against DVWA's Reflected XSS module with the security level set to Low.

The following payload was submitted:

<script>alert('SentinelForge-Test')</script>

The test confirmed:

The JavaScript alert fired in the browser, confirming that the reflected XSS payload executed.
Rule 100012 fired in Wazuh Discover.
The payload was visible in the expanded alert's URL field.
Step 3: Payload variation testing

Additional payloads were tested:

<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS2')>

and:

<svg onload=alert('XSS3')>

These variations were used to verify that the underlying detection was not dependent only on the literal <script> string, but could identify a broader XSS-pattern group.

Troubleshooting
Problem: Brute-force rule fired on every single failure

Cause: The initial rule had no frequency, timeframe, or same_source_ip correlation.

Solution: Frequency and source-IP correlation were added to the rule:

frequency="6"
timeframe="120"

along with:

<if_matched_sid>5760</if_matched_sid>
<same_source_ip />

This changed the detection from identifying individual authentication failures to identifying a repeated brute-force pattern.

Verification Checklist

SSH brute-force rule fires only on the correlated burst, not a single failure.

Brute-force rule is tagged with T1110.001.

XSS rule fires on a DVWA reflected XSS payload.

XSS detection was confirmed against multiple payload variants.

Both rules are visible in Wazuh Discover with the correct rule ID and description.

Lessons Learned
1. A rule that fires is not automatically a correct detection

The initial SSH rule successfully generated alerts, but it detected individual authentication failures rather than a brute-force pattern.

Matching the pattern — repeated failures within a short period — rather than the symptom — one failed login — is what makes the detection more useful and reduces noise.

2. MITRE technique tags should match the actual detection

MITRE ATT&CK mappings should reflect what the rule actually detects.

Labeling a single authentication failure as T1110.001 would be misleading because one failed login does not necessarily indicate password guessing.

The corrected rule detects repeated credential failures from the same source, making T1110.001 appropriate for the intended detection.

3. Test against payload variations

Testing only one canonical XSS payload can create false confidence.

Testing multiple payload structures helps establish that the detection is identifying a broader XSS pattern rather than matching one specific string.

